# Collections: [abc], [^abc], ranges, [:class:] expressions, escapes inside [], and \_[] with line breaks.
# Buffer markup escapes [ and ] as \[ and \].

=== [] matches any one character in it
ref:    pattern.txt */[]*
quote:  It matches any single character in the collection.
given:  |xaybz
keys:   :s/[xyz]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(x)a(y)b(z)

=== [^] matches any character not in it
ref:    pattern.txt *E944*
quote:  "\"[^xyz]\" matches anything but 'x', 'y' and 'z'."
given:  |xaybz
keys:   :s/[^xyz]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |x(a)y(b)z

=== [a-z] is a range
ref:    pattern.txt *E944*
quote:  "\"[0-9]\" matches any decimal digit."
given:  |a1B2c
keys:   :s/[a-z0-9]\+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(a1)B(2c)

=== [a-zA-Z]$ matches a letter at the end of the line
ref:    pattern.txt */[]*
quote:  any alphabetic character at the end of a line
given:  |ab1 cD
keys:   :s/[a-zA-Z]$/(&)/<CR>
expect: |ab1 c(D)

=== a range of non-ASCII characters
ref:    pattern.txt *E944*
quote:  Non-ASCII characters can be used, but the character values must not be more than 256 apart in the old regexp engine.
given:  |aéèz
keys:   :s/[à-ÿ]\+/(&)/<CR>
expect: |a(éè)z

=== [^] does not match an end-of-line
ref:    pattern.txt */[\n]*
quote:  Without the "\_" or "\n" the collection does not match an end-of-line.
given:
  |a
  b
keys:   :%s/a[^x]b/X/e<CR>
expect:
  |a
  b

=== [\n] in a collection matches an end-of-line
ref:    pattern.txt */[\n]*
quote:  The same can be done by including "\n" in the collection.
given:
  |a
  b
keys:   :%s/a[\n]b/X/<CR>
expect:
  |X

=== \_[] adds the end-of-line to a collection
ref:    pattern.txt */[\n]*
quote:  With "\_" prepended the collection also includes the end-of-line.
given:
  |a
  b
keys:   :%s/a\_[xyz]b/X/<CR>
expect:
  |X

=== \_[^ab] matches the end-of-line too
ref:    pattern.txt */[\n]*
quote:  Thus "\_[^ab]" matches the end-of-line and any character but "a" and "b".
given:
  |x
  y x-y
keys:   :%s/x\_[^ab]y/Z/g<CR>
expect:
  |Z Z

=== [:alpha:] matches ASCII letters
ref:    pattern.txt *[:alpha:]*
quote:  ASCII letters
given:  |ab1c
keys:   :s/[[:alpha:]]\+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(ab)1(c)

=== [:digit:] matches digits
ref:    pattern.txt *[:digit:]*
quote:  decimal digits '0' to '9'
given:  |ab12c
keys:   :s/[[:digit:]]\+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |ab(12)c

=== [:alnum:] matches letters and digits
ref:    pattern.txt *[:alnum:]*
quote:  ASCII letters and digits
given:  |a1-b2
keys:   :s/[[:alnum:]]\+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(a1)-(b2)

=== [:space:] matches white space
ref:    pattern.txt *[:space:]*
quote:  whitespace characters: space, tab, CR, NL, vertical tab, form feed
given:  |a b	c
keys:   :s/[[:space:]]/_/g<CR>
expect: |a_b_c

=== [:blank:] matches space and tab
ref:    pattern.txt *[:blank:]*
quote:  space and tab
given:  |a b	c
keys:   :s/[[:blank:]]/_/g<CR>
expect: |a_b_c

=== [:upper:] matches uppercase letters
ref:    pattern.txt *[:upper:]*
quote:  uppercase letters
given:  |aBcD
keys:   :s/[[:upper:]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(B)c(D)

=== [:lower:] matches multibyte lowercase letters
ref:    pattern.txt *[:fname:]*
quote:  These items only work for 8-bit characters, except [:lower:] and [:upper:] also work for multibyte characters when using the new regexp engine.
given:  |ÉéAa
keys:   :s/[[:lower:]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |É(é)A(a)

=== [:upper:] matches multibyte uppercase letters
ref:    pattern.txt *[:fname:]*
quote:  These items only work for 8-bit characters, except [:lower:] and [:upper:] also work for multibyte characters when using the new regexp engine.
given:  |ÉéAa
keys:   :s/[[:upper:]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(É)é(A)a

=== [:punct:] matches punctuation
ref:    pattern.txt *[:punct:]*
quote:  ASCII punctuation characters
given:  |a,b!c
keys:   :s/[[:punct:]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(,)b(!)c

=== [:xdigit:] matches hex digits
ref:    pattern.txt *[:xdigit:]*
quote:  hexadecimal digits: 0-9, a-f, A-F
given:  |fg0
keys:   :s/[[:xdigit:]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(f)g(0)

=== [:keyword:] is the same as \k
ref:    pattern.txt *[:keyword:]*
quote:  keyword character (same as "\k")
given:  |café-x
keys:   :s/[[:keyword:]]\+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(café)-(x)

=== class expressions combine with other characters
ref:    pattern.txt *[:fname:]*
quote:  "\"[-./[:alnum:]_~]\\+\".  That is, a list of at least one character, each of which is either '-', '.', '/', alphabetic, numeric, '_' or '~'."
given:  |see ./a-b_c~ ok
keys:   :s/[-./[:alnum:]_~]\+/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(see) (./a-b_c~) (ok)

=== ] first in a collection is literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  For ']' you can also make it the first character (following a possible "^"):  "[]xyz]" or "[^]xyz]".
given:  |a\]b
keys:   :s/[]b]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(\])(b)

=== \] inside a collection is literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  To include a literal ']', '^', '-' or '\' in the collection, put a backslash before it:
given:  |a\]b
keys:   :s/[x\]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(\])b

=== - at the end of a collection is literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  For '-' you can also make it the first or last character: "[-xyz]", "[^-xyz]" or "[xyz-]".
given:  |a-b
keys:   :s/[x-]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(-)b

=== \^ inside a collection is literal
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  To include a literal ']', '^', '-' or '\' in the collection, put a backslash before it:
given:  |a\^b
keys:   :s/[\^b]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(\^)(b)

=== \\ inside a collection is a backslash
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  To include a literal ']', '^', '-' or '\' in the collection, put a backslash before it:
given:  |a\b
keys:   :s/[\\]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |a(\)b

=== \t inside a collection is a tab
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  The following translations are accepted when the 'l' flag is not included in 'cpoptions':
given:  |a	b
keys:   :s/[\t]/T/g<CR>
expect: |aTb

=== \d123 inside a collection is a decimal character code
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  decimal number of character
given:  |bab
keys:   :s/[\d97]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |b(a)b

=== \u inside a collection is a multibyte character code
ref:    pattern.txt */\]*
quote:  hex. number of multibyte character up to 0xffff
given:  |5€ 6$
keys:   :s/[€$]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |5(€) 6($)

=== an unclosed [ is a literal [
ref:    pattern.txt *E769*
quote:  When the ']' is not there Vim will not give an error message but assume no collection is used.  Useful to search for '['.
given:  |x a\[b
keys:   /a[b<CR>
expect: x |a\[b

=== :s/[/x/ treats the whole command as the pattern
ref:    pattern.txt *E769*
quote:  "\":s/[/x/\" searches for \"[/x\" and replaces it with nothing."
given:  |a\[/x/b
keys:   :s/[/x/<CR>
expect: |ab

=== [[=e=]] matches equivalent characters
ref:    pattern.txt */[[=*
quote:  An equivalence class.  This means that characters are matched that have almost the same meaning, e.g., when ignoring accents.
given:  |eéèx
keys:   :s/[[=e=]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |(e)(é)(è)x

=== [[.a.]] is a collation element
ref:    pattern.txt */[[.*
quote:  A collation element.  This currently simply accepts a single character in the form:
given:  |bab
keys:   :s/[[.a.]]/(&)/g<CR>
expect: |b(a)b
